.\" Manual page for git-refresh.
.\" Hand-written roff. There is no generator; edit this file directly.
.TH GIT\-REFRESH 1 "2026-08-18" "git-refresh" "Git Extras"
.
.SH NAME
git\-refresh \- fetch, sync the fork, and rebase each branch onto the right base
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git refresh
.RI [ base ]
.RB [ \-i ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-\-all | \-\-stack ]
.RB [ \-\-please ]
.RB [ \-\-prune ]
.RB [ \-\-sync ]
.RB [ \-\-doctor ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-icons ]
.RB [ \-\-color [ =\c
.IR when ]]
.br
.B git refresh
.B \-h
.PP
Also installed as
.BR git\-refresh ,
and reachable as
.B git refresh
without an alias: git looks for a
.B git\-refresh
in
.B PATH
before it consults aliases.
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git refresh
fetches every remote, fast\-forwards origin's default branch from
.BR upstream 's
when an
.B upstream
remote exists, works out the base each branch belongs on, and rebases onto it.
.PP
The right base for a branch is the target of its open pull request, not
.IR origin/ <same\-name>.
A branch stacked on another therefore lands on the branch below it rather than
flattening onto the default branch. Where no pull request exists yet, the base
recorded by
.BR git\-new\-worktree (1)
in
.IB branch. <name> .base
stands in.
.PP
Bases are resolved in this order, first hit wins:
.IP 1. 4
The base branch of the branch's open pull request, read with
.BR gh (1).
This is the authority once a pull request exists.
.IP 2. 4
.IB branch. <name> .base
from git config. If that branch is gone from origin \(em merged and deleted
while you worked \(em it is followed one hop to whatever it merged into.
.IP 3. 4
The default branch. On a single branch this is assumed outright; under
.B \-\-all
it is assumed only where the shape of the history agrees, because guessing
wrong there flattens a whole unopened stack onto the default branch.
.PP
A branch none of those answer for is reported as
.B "no base"
and left alone;
.B \-\-doctor
says which piece is missing and offers the command that records it.
.PP
Every rebase targets
.IR origin/ <base>,
never a local branch of the same name. That is why a stack needs its lower
branches pushed before the ones above them land on anything new, and why
.B \-\-all
sorts branches topologically by base and processes them bottom\-up.
.PP
One branch is refreshed by default: the one you are standing in.
.B \-\-all
does every worktree and is assumed at the bare repository root, where there is
no branch the command could mean. Single\-branch mode never looks at the
worktree layout, so an ordinary clone behaves exactly like a worktree checkout.
.PP
Neither mode requires the layout the companion scripts create.
.B \-\-all
finds its worktrees with
.BR "git worktree list" ,
so it walks any arrangement, including worktrees made with plain
.B "git worktree add"
and placed wherever you chose. Only
.B git\-clone\-for\-worktrees
and
.B git\-new\-worktree
decide where a directory goes.
.PP
.B \-\-all
and
.B \-\-stack
run every rebase with
.B rerere.enabled=false
whatever your config says: those rebases are speculative and are thrown away the
moment they conflict, so they have no business recording a resolution you never
reviewed or replaying one across twenty branches unattended. On a single branch
rerere is left exactly as you configured it.
.PP
Conflicts are handled differently in the two modes. Under
.B \-\-all
or
.B \-\-stack
a conflicting rebase is aborted and handed back as a command to run, so a pass
over twenty worktrees cannot leave six of them wedged mid\-rebase. On a single
branch the conflict is left in place to resolve, since aborting would only mean
running the identical rebase by hand to reach the identical conflict.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I base
Rebase onto this instead of the resolved base. A bare name is looked up under
origin first, so
.B git refresh foo
means
.BR origin/foo .
One branch only; it is an error alongside
.B \-\-all
or
.BR \-\-stack .
.TP
.B \-\-all
Refresh every worktree, in stack order. Assumed at the bare repository root.
.TP
.B \-\-stack
Refresh only the branches stacked with this one, reached through pull request
bases in both directions and processed bottom\-up. The branches above are
included because they are the ones a rebase here would strand. Requires a
worktree to start from, and cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-all .
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Follow the rebase with an interactive pass from the merge base. One branch only.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show what would happen. The fetch still runs \(em with stale remote\-tracking
refs a dry run would answer about the origin of the last real fetch \(em but
nothing else moves: no rebase, no push, no worktree removed, and origin's
default branch is not fast\-forwarded from upstream. Conflicts are predicted
with
.B "git merge\-tree"
rather than met.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print git's own fetch, rebase and push output as well as the table.
.TP
.BR \-\-push\-with\-lease ", " \-\-please
Push each branch this run rebased, refusing any push that would discard commits
you have not seen. On by default; see
.B CONFIGURATION
and
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-push\-with\-lease ", " \-\-no\-please
Do not push. Overrides
.BR refresh.pushWithLease .
.TP
.B \-\-prune
Remove worktrees whose branch is gone from origin and shows evidence of having
been merged, and delete the branch with them. Never touches a worktree with
changes, the one you are standing in, the main worktree, the default branch, or
one with no sign of having been merged; those are reported instead. A branch
that was never pushed is never pruned. Off by default.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-prune
Do not prune. Overrides
.BR refresh.pruneWorktrees .
.TP
.B \-\-sync
Adopt
.IR origin/ <branch>
before rebasing, for a branch that was rewritten elsewhere and holds nothing
origin lacks. It is replayed onto the remote rather than reset onto it, so work
is not lost even where that judgement is wrong. A branch with commits of its own
under a rewritten remote is reported as
.B "diverged from origin"
and left alone. On by default.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-sync
Do not adopt the remote. Overrides
.BR refresh.syncStale .
.TP
.B \-\-doctor
Recommend what to do about everything the run reported: conflicting files by
name, unpushed commits by subject, branches with no base, and worktrees held
back from pruning. Implies
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
so a conflict is predicted rather than met. Diagnoses one branch; it is an error
alongside
.B \-\-all
or
.BR \-\-stack ,
and it needs a worktree to stand in.
.TP
.B \-\-icons
Use emoji in the status column and the messages. On by default.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-icons
Drop the emoji. Overrides
.BR refresh.icons .
Severity is still carried by colour and by the words themselves.
.TP
.BI \-\-color [ =when ]
Colour the status column by severity.
.I when
is
.B auto
(a terminal only, with
.B NO_COLOR
unset and
.B TERM
not
.BR dumb ),
.BR always ,
or
.BR never .
Bare
.B \-\-color
means
.BR always .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Same as
.BR \-\-color=never .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage summary and exit 0.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of options. The next argument is taken as
.IR base .
.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Command line first, then git config, then the default below. An explicit
.BI \-\-no\- x
always wins over config.
.PP
A key that is set to something git cannot read as a boolean is a fatal error,
not a silent "off". That is deliberate for
.BR refresh.pruneWorktrees ,
where a typo would otherwise quietly disable a setting that removes worktrees.
.TP
.B refresh.pushWithLease
Boolean.
.BR "Default true" :
pushing happens unless the key is set
.BR false .
Same as
.BR \-\-please ;
turn it off for one run with
.BR \-\-no\-please .
.TP
.B refresh.syncStale
Boolean.
.BR "Default true" .
Same as
.BR \-\-sync ;
.B \-\-no\-sync
for one run.
.TP
.B refresh.pruneWorktrees
Boolean.
.BR "Default false" ,
because pruning removes worktrees and deletes their branches. Same as
.BR \-\-prune ;
.B \-\-no\-prune
for one run.
.TP
.B refresh.icons
Boolean.
.BR "Default true" .
Same as
.BR \-\-icons ;
.B \-\-no\-icons
for one run.
.TP
.B color.refresh
.BR auto ", " always " or " never .
.BR "Default auto" .
Same as
.BR \-\-color= when.
.TP
.B rebase.autoStash
Boolean, git's own key, not one of this tool's.
.BR "Default false" ,
as git defines it, and
.B honoured
here: with it set
.BR true ,
a worktree with uncommitted tracked changes is rebased anyway and git stashes
and reapplies them, instead of the branch being skipped under
.B \-\-all
or refused on a single branch. If reapplying the stash conflicts, the branch is
reported as
.B "autostash conflicted"
and the changes are left conflicted in the worktree \(em they are still in the
stash too, so drop it once they are resolved.
.TP
.BI branch. <name> .base
The base recorded by
.BR git\-new\-worktree (1)
when it created the branch, and the fallback used when the branch has no open
pull request. Safe to set by hand:
.B "git config branch.<name>.base main"
.TP
.BI branch. <name> .remote
Read by
.B \-\-prune
only. It survives the remote branch being deleted, so it is the evidence that a
branch was pushed at some point; a branch without it is never pruned.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set to anything,
.B \-\-color=auto
produces no colour.
.TP
.B TERM
.B dumb
(or unset) disables colour under
.BR \-\-color=auto .
.TP
.BR GIT_DIR ", " GIT_WORK_TREE ", " GIT_INDEX_FILE ", " GIT_PREFIX
Unset before anything else runs. The git alias entry point exports
.B GIT_DIR
pointing at whichever worktree git was invoked from, which would otherwise
retarget every git call in the run.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IB repo /.git
The bare repository, when the worktree layout is in use. Worktrees are its
siblings,
.IB repo / <branch>\fR.
.TP
.I $TMPDIR
A temporary directory is created per run for the plan and the per\-branch
findings, and removed on exit.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Everything the run touched is on its base.
.TP
.B 1
A rebase conflicted or a push was refused. Also the status of any fatal error:
not inside a repository, more than one base given, a config value that is not a
boolean, an unresolvable
.BR origin/HEAD ,
or a flag combination that names two different sets of branches.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: an unknown option.
.PP
Suitable for a
.B &&
chain or a CI step.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Rebase the branch you are standing in onto its base, and push it:
.PP
.EX
git refresh
.EE
.PP
Look at every worktree without changing anything:
.PP
.EX
git refresh \-\-all \-\-dry\-run
.EE
.PP
Restack one chain of pull requests, bottom\-up, pushing as it goes:
.PP
.EX
cd add\-billing\-writes
git refresh \-\-stack \-\-please
.EE
.PP
Find out what to do about the branch you are on:
.PP
.EX
git refresh \-\-doctor
.EE
.PP
Refresh every worktree and clear out the ones that are finished:
.PP
.EX
git refresh \-\-all \-\-prune
.EE
.PP
Rebase onto a base of your choosing rather than the resolved one:
.PP
.EX
git refresh main
.EE
.PP
Make pruning the default, and turn it off for one run:
.PP
.EX
git config \-\-global refresh.pruneWorktrees true
git refresh \-\-all \-\-no\-prune
.EE
.
.SH NOTES
.SS Why the lease is explicit
A rebase rewrites every commit, so publishing one means force\-pushing. Bare
.B \-\-force\-with\-lease
is vacuous here: it checks the push against the remote\-tracking ref that the
fetch at the top of this run has just updated, so it would approve overwriting a
colleague's commit that arrived in that very fetch.
.B \-\-force\-if\-includes
is no help either \(em it reads a branch reflog, and a bare repository keeps
none.
.B \-\-please
therefore snapshots where each branch stood on origin
.I before
the fetch and pushes with that value as the lease. If someone pushed in between,
the push is refused and reported.
.SS Squash merges
When a base branch is squash\-merged, nothing git has for recognising "this work
is already upstream" survives: patch\-ids differ, ancestry is broken, and both
tips carry the same change by different routes. The branch above it will replay
its parent's commits onto a base that already contains their content. The fix is
.B "git rebase \-\-onto"
by hand and, upstream of the tooling, enabling rebase merges on the repository.
Expect
.B \-\-doctor
to be optimistic about such a branch: it merges tips, and a squashed base looks
clean right up until the rebase replays.
.
